    <dependencies>
        <!--
        App dependency: openregister. Recorded as prose, not as an element. The
        App Store schema (info.xsd) has no app child under dependencies, so
        shipping one fails App Store validation; Nextcloud's DependencyAnalyzer
        does not read it either (it handles only php, database, command, lib,
        os, architecture and the server version), so the element never enforced
        anything at install time. src/manifest.json is where the requirement is
        declared machine-readably.
        -->
        <php min-version="8.3"/>
        <!--
        min-version is 32 by product decision: the fleet standardises on
        Nextcloud 32 so it can require PHP 8.3, which `<php min-version>` above
        already declares. opencatalogi, openconnector and docudesk are already
        at 32; this brings Buildiq in line.

        The openconnector#1172/#1173 dependency-floor rule ALSO requires 32 now.
        That rule says an app's min-version must be >= the max of every app
        dependency's floor, and the openregister dependency noted above is a
        HARD dependency. openregister's floor has moved three times in two days:
        raised to 32 in openregister#2378, reverted to 28 in #2380 (after #2372
        removed every eager reference to its ContextChat provider), and raised
        back to 32 in openregister#2384, merged 2026-08-08T10:45Z and deliberately
        fleet-wide. Re-measured against the canonical repo immediately before this
        merge, `ConductionNL/openregister@development` declares a nextcloud
        min-version of 32 (max-version 34).

        Deliberately spelled out in prose, with no angle brackets anywhere in
        this comment: writing that dependency's declaration as a literal XML
        element here would add a second match to the nextcloud-element COUNT
        that check:nc-floor asserts, and the check reads raw file text, comments
        included. To a regex a commented-out example is indistinguishable from a
        real declaration — that is precisely how procest's and doriath's floors
        were misread as 28 by a reviewer grepping this file.

        So 32 here is not merely >= the dependency floor, it is EQUAL to it: 28
        would now advertise an App Store range this app cannot deliver, because
        below 32 openregister refuses to install and every Buildiq entity is
        an OpenRegister object.

        Note that `openregister@main` still declares 28 — the fleet is mid-rollout
        and main trails development by ~1000 commits. Do not read that as a
        contradiction, and do not lower this floor to match it.

        The comment that stood here previously asserted "min-version is 32" while
        the attribute said 28: #146 lowered the value and left the prose behind.
        That is the failure this block is written to avoid — a floor comment is
        only worth anything if it is true at MERGE time, so re-measure the
        dependency's floor rather than repeating what this text says.
        `check:nc-floor` (scripts/check-nc-floor.js, wired into the
        frontend-checks matrix) enforces the value; only the prose can drift.

        The floor is enforced at INSTALL time, so it must not exceed what this
        repo's own CI installs on. `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` runs
        `nextcloud-test-refs: ["stable32", "stable33"]` — stable31 was already
        removed — so no CI leg targets NC < 32.

        max-version stays 34, which is the fleet value everywhere except
        openconnector (35).
        -->
        <nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>
    </dependencies>

