    <repair-steps>
        <post-migration>
            <!--
                MUST run before InitializeSettings, which triggers the register
                import. The register slug moves with the app id (`openbuild` ->
                `buildiq`), together with `x-openregister.app` — for a
                `type: application` configuration that field IS a register slug
                (ImportHandler::autoCreateRegisterIfApplication reads
                `$xOpenregister['app'] ?? $appId`). OpenRegister resolves a
                register by slug and its not-found branch CREATES a second,
                empty register rather than failing, so an import that ran first
                would fork the register and leave every stored object behind on
                the old row, silently.

                It moves no data: an object is bound to its register by NUMERIC
                id. Idempotent, non-destructive, refuses rather than merges when
                both slugs already exist.

                SCOPED TO THIS APP'S OWN REGISTER. The per-generated-app
                registers `openbuild-{slug}[-{version}]` are deliberately NOT in
                its map: those names are minted per generated app, stored on the
                app, and resolved with `registerMapper->find('openbuild-' . $slug)`
                — and the credential broker holds `allowedApps` entries against
                the matching `openbuild-{slug}` virtual app ids. They move only
                in a pass that moves the minted credentials with them.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\MigrateRegisterSlug</step>
            <!--
                SIBLING OF MigrateRegisterSlug, AND NEITHER COVERS THE OTHER.
                OpenRegister resolves the two halves of a configuration by
                DIFFERENT keys: a REGISTER by slug alone (RegisterMapper::find),
                a SCHEMA by the PAIR —
                SchemaMapper::findByApplicationAndSlug(slug, application).

                This app now passes `appId: Application::APP_ID` = `buildiq`,
                while every schema it already owns still carries
                `application = openbuild`, so the pair matches nothing — and
                ImportHandler's not-found branch is not an error path, it is the
                "create a new one" path. The import InitializeSettings triggers
                would build a SECOND, EMPTY schema set under the new application
                id while every stored object stayed bound to the old rows.
                Nothing errors; the app renders empty collections.

                MUST run before InitializeSettings, for the same reason
                MigrateRegisterSlug does — the import is the thing that forks.

                Measured on a live install: 17 schemas still under `openbuild`,
                and zero of their slugs collide with a twin under `buildiq`, so
                all 17 move.

                Where a slug DOES already have a twin under the new application
                id it refuses rather than merges: two rows sharing
                (application, slug) means findByApplicationAndSlug(), which caps
                at one row, silently picks one and the other's objects become
                unreachable. That is a decision about data, not a migration.

                Never deletes a schema, and never throws — it runs under
                <install>, where an escaping exception aborts the install and
                the app never enables.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
            <!--
                AFTER MigrateSchemaApplicationId and BEFORE InitializeSettings.
                A schema slug is global per organisation, so this app's 6-field
                `agent` workspace pointer and hermiq's 41-field AI agent both
                answered to a bare `agent`. Renaming the row first means the
                import updates it in place instead of creating a second schema
                and orphaning every object on the first.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\RenameAgentSchemaSlug</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\InitializeSettings</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\MigrateToVersionedModel</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\MigrateAppOverridesToHybrid</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\PopulateApplicationPermissions</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\SeedApplicationTemplates</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\FlagRegistryTokenSensitive</step>
            <!--
                Must run AFTER the register sync that adds the English columns, so
                it can detect the "mapper already added an empty column" case and
                back-fill rather than rename. Idempotent; safe to re-run.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\RenameDutchRuleColumns</step>
        </post-migration>
        <install>
            <!--
                MUST run before InitializeSettings, which triggers the register
                import. The register slug moves with the app id (`openbuild` ->
                `buildiq`), together with `x-openregister.app` — for a
                `type: application` configuration that field IS a register slug
                (ImportHandler::autoCreateRegisterIfApplication reads
                `$xOpenregister['app'] ?? $appId`). OpenRegister resolves a
                register by slug and its not-found branch CREATES a second,
                empty register rather than failing, so an import that ran first
                would fork the register and leave every stored object behind on
                the old row, silently.

                It moves no data: an object is bound to its register by NUMERIC
                id. Idempotent, non-destructive, refuses rather than merges when
                both slugs already exist.

                SCOPED TO THIS APP'S OWN REGISTER. The per-generated-app
                registers `openbuild-{slug}[-{version}]` are deliberately NOT in
                its map: those names are minted per generated app, stored on the
                app, and resolved with `registerMapper->find('openbuild-' . $slug)`
                — and the credential broker holds `allowedApps` entries against
                the matching `openbuild-{slug}` virtual app ids. They move only
                in a pass that moves the minted credentials with them.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\MigrateRegisterSlug</step>
            <!--
                SIBLING OF MigrateRegisterSlug, AND NEITHER COVERS THE OTHER.
                OpenRegister resolves the two halves of a configuration by
                DIFFERENT keys: a REGISTER by slug alone (RegisterMapper::find),
                a SCHEMA by the PAIR —
                SchemaMapper::findByApplicationAndSlug(slug, application).

                This app now passes `appId: Application::APP_ID` = `buildiq`,
                while every schema it already owns still carries
                `application = openbuild`, so the pair matches nothing — and
                ImportHandler's not-found branch is not an error path, it is the
                "create a new one" path. The import InitializeSettings triggers
                would build a SECOND, EMPTY schema set under the new application
                id while every stored object stayed bound to the old rows.
                Nothing errors; the app renders empty collections.

                MUST run before InitializeSettings, for the same reason
                MigrateRegisterSlug does — the import is the thing that forks.

                Measured on a live install: 17 schemas still under `openbuild`,
                and zero of their slugs collide with a twin under `buildiq`, so
                all 17 move.

                Where a slug DOES already have a twin under the new application
                id it refuses rather than merges: two rows sharing
                (application, slug) means findByApplicationAndSlug(), which caps
                at one row, silently picks one and the other's objects become
                unreachable. That is a decision about data, not a migration.

                Never deletes a schema, and never throws — it runs under
                <install>, where an escaping exception aborts the install and
                the app never enables.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
            <!--
                AFTER MigrateSchemaApplicationId and BEFORE InitializeSettings.
                A schema slug is global per organisation, so this app's 6-field
                `agent` workspace pointer and hermiq's 41-field AI agent both
                answered to a bare `agent`. Renaming the row first means the
                import updates it in place instead of creating a second schema
                and orphaning every object on the first.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\RenameAgentSchemaSlug</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\InitializeSettings</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\MigrateToVersionedModel</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\MigrateAppOverridesToHybrid</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\PopulateApplicationPermissions</step>
            <step>OCA\Buildiq\Repair\SeedApplicationTemplates</step>
        </install>
    </repair-steps>

